<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch05_P05_Ex10_factor_cases</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The exercise above asks you to prove that \(n^7 - n\) is divisible by 7. One way to start:
\[n^7 - n = n(n - 1)(n + 1)(n^2 + n + 1)(n^2 - n + 1)\]
(expand to check). Every integer is \(n = 7k + r\) with \(r\) one of \(0, 1, \ldots, 6\), and in each case one of the five factors is a multiple of 7.</p>
<p>Three rows are done. Type the factor that is a multiple of 7 for each of the other four.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>\(r\)</th><th>factor that is a multiple of 7 when \(n = 7k + r\)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>0</td><td>\(n = 7k\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>\(n - 1 = 7k\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td><div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td><div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div></td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(n + 1 = 7k + 7 = 7(k + 1)\)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>For \(n = 7k + r\), each factor equals its value at \(n = r\) plus a multiple of 7; for example
\[(7k + r)^2 + (7k + r) + 1 = 7(7k^2 + 2kr + k) + (r^2 + r + 1).\]
So a factor is a multiple of 7 exactly when its value at \(n = r\) is.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>\(r\)</th><th>factor</th><th>value at \(n = r\)</th><th>as a multiple of 7</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>0</td><td>\(n\)</td><td>0</td><td>\(7k\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>\(n - 1\)</td><td>0</td><td>\(7k\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>\(n^2 + n + 1\)</td><td>7</td><td>\(7(7k^2 + 5k + 1)\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>\(n^2 - n + 1\)</td><td>7</td><td>\(7(7k^2 + 5k + 1)\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>\(n^2 + n + 1\)</td><td>21</td><td>\(7(7k^2 + 9k + 3)\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>\(n^2 - n + 1\)</td><td>21</td><td>\(7(7k^2 + 9k + 3)\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>\(n + 1\)</td><td>7</td><td>\(7(k + 1)\)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In every case one factor is a multiple of 7, so \(n^7 - n\) is a multiple of 7.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
